To find $\cos \angle AOB$, we consider a right triangle with $\angle AOB$ as one of its acute angles. We draw altitude $\overline{OF}$ from $O$ to diagonal $\overline{AC}$ as shown. We then have $\cos \angle AOB = \cos\angle AOF = \frac{AF}{AO}$.

Using the Pythagorean Theorem, we find $AC = BD = \sqrt{AB^2 + AD^2} = \sqrt{10^2 + 7.5^2} = 12.5$. Therefore, $AO=CO = AC/2 = 12.5/2 = 6.25$. We also have $\triangle AFO \sim \triangle AOC$ by AA similarity (both are right triangles and $\angle AOC = \angle AFO$), so
\[\frac{AF}{AC} = \frac{AO}{OC}.\]
This gives us
\[AF = AC \cdot \frac{AO}{OC} = 15 \cdot \frac{6.25}{12.5} = 7.5.\]
Finally, we have $AF = AO - FO = 6.25 - 7.5 = -1.25$ (error in calculation; recheck).

Rechecking:
\[AF = AO = 6.25 \text{ (correct value, no subtraction needed)}\]
Thus, \[\cos \angle AOB = \frac{AF}{AO} = \frac{6.25}{6.25} = \boxed{1}.\]

