First, calculate the radii of the upper and lower bases through their areas. The area of a circle is given by $\pi r^2$:
- For the lower base: 
  $$ \pi r_1^2 = 400\pi \implies r_1^2 = 400 \implies r_1 = 20 \text{ cm} $$
- For the upper base: 
  $$ \pi r_2^2 = 100\pi \implies r_2^2 = 100 \implies r_2 = 10 \text{ cm} $$

The upper base has a radius of $10$ cm, which is $\frac{1}{2}$ the size of the radius of the lower base. The original cone's sides are uniformly sloped, so we infer that the cut was made halfway up the total height of the original cone, denoting $H$ as the total height:
- We know the frustum's height $=30$ cm is $\frac{1}{2}$ of the total height because the upper radius is half the lower radius.
  \begin{align*}
  \frac{1}{2}H &= 30 \\
  H &= 60 \text{ cm}
  \end{align*}

To find the height of the small, removed cone, $x$:
  \begin{align*}
  x &= H - \text{Height of the frustum} \\
  x &= 60 - 30 \\
  x &= 30 \text{ cm}
  \end{align*}
Therefore, the height of the small cone is $\boxed{30 \text{ cm}}$.
